<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.2: Standard and Factored Forms of Perfect Squares</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02351</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.2: Standard and Factored Forms of Perfect Squares</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a2d814f6-f69e-40ab-a8c4-a92558eaf183</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the factored form of \(x^2+6x+9\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((x+3)^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the factored form of \(x^2−10x+25\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((x−5)^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the standard form of \((x−7)^2\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(x^2−14x+49\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="b09a9116-d201-4895-a4be-a2f77bd179d2" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="192cf81b-d5a2-40e1-8371-c1dc947f33fe" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="100">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(c\) in the expression \(x^2−20x+ c\)?</para>
      <para>Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 100</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="54de037c-cf37-4094-a566-86908cd0c14f" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="10">
    <problem>
      <para>If \(x^2−20x+ c\) is factored to be \((x\; -\;\;\;)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression?</para>
      <para>Enter the missing value of the factored form.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 10</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a9b00ee4-f9d9-4f3f-887a-15186f558c2b" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="64">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the value of \(c\) in the expression \(x^2+16x+ c\)?</para>
      <para>Enter the value of \(c\).</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 64</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3d0250f2-4dbd-43fe-924d-38870521d864" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="8">
    <problem>
      <para>If \(x^2+16x+ c\) is factored to be \((x\; -\;\;\;)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression?</para>
      <para>Enter the missing value of the factored form.</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 8</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the value of \(c\) in the expression \(x^2+7x+ c\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(\frac{49}{4}\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>If \(x^2+7x+ c\) is factored to be \((x\; -\;\;\;)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(\frac72\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>What is the value of \(c\) in the expression \(x^2+bx+ c\)?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((\frac{b}{2})^2\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>If \(x^2+bx+ c\) is factored to be \((x\; -\;\;\;)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression?</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(\frac{b}{2}\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>

<para>Notice that finding the value of \(c\) helped to create a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">perfect square trinomial</term>. This is called <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>.</para>

<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Building Character: Intellectual Humility</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0943d627452fc6cc10570e6cded146178d40e44d"/></media></figure><div>
      <para class="os-raise-mb-0">When you approach life with intellectual humility, you open your mind to learning. You are able to learn from opposing views and have more constructive discussions, even when you disagree. </para>
      <para>Think about yourself. Are these statements true for you?</para>
      <list>
        <item>
          I question my own opinions, positions, and viewpoints because they could be wrong.
        </item>
        <item>
          In the face of conflicting evidence, I am open to changing my opinions.
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>
</content>
</document>
